    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To determine the type of curve on which all the plotted points \\((x, y) = \\left( \\frac{t + 1}{t}, \\frac{t - 1}{t} \\right)\\) for \\( t \\neq 0 \\) lie, we need to analyze the relationship between \\(x\\) and \\(y\\).\n\nFirst, let's express \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) in terms of \\(t\\):\n\\[\nx = \\frac{t + 1}{t}\n\\]\n\\[\ny = \\frac{t - 1}{t}\n\\]\n\nWe can simplify these expressions:\n\\[\nx = 1 + \\frac{1}{t}\n\\]\n\\[\ny = 1 - \\frac{1}{t}\n\\]\n\nNext, let's manipulate these equations to express \\(x\\) in terms of \\(y\\) and vice versa. From the second equation, we can isolate \\(\\frac{1}{t}\\):\n\\[\ny = 1 - \\frac{1}{t} \\implies \\frac{1}{t} = 1 - y\n\\]\n\nNow, substitute this expression for \\(\\frac{1}{t}\\) into the first equation:\n\\[\nx = 1 + \\frac{1}{t} \\implies x = 1 + 1 - y\n\\]\n\\[\nx = 2 - y\n\\]\n\nThis equation \\(x = 2 - y\\) shows that \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) are related by a linear equation. Specifically, the equation \\(x = 2 - y\\) describes a line in the \\(xy\\)-plane.\n\nThus, all the plotted points \\((x, y) = \\left( \\frac{t + 1}{t}, \\frac{t - 1}{t} \\right)\\) lie on a line.\n\nThe correct option is \\(\\boxed{\\text{A}}\\).",
