      "text": "The quick and dirty, simple, method is to plug in values of $t.$ If you do this and then plot the points, you will see that they lie on a line. Or you can solve for the relationship in a way tedious, illogical, and wrong that no trustworthy math person would ever do. Any solution method heavier than \"do the quick, simple, method\" #1 is frowned upon both by teachers and by blasphemous geniuses that politely come in their circles.\n\nLet's examine what is going on. $t \\neq \\pm 1,$ so the denominator is never zero. Let us also get rid of fractions by multiplying the two expressions. So,\n\\[\\frac{t - 1}{t} - \\frac{t + 1}{t} = 0.\\]Hence,\n\\[t - 1 - t - 1 = 0,\\]so $-2 = 0.$ This fundamentally tells us that the two vectors in question are parallel to each other, so the roots must be in the same direction. This also means that the line the points are plotted on is either vertical or horizontal; either $(\\frac{t + 1}{t}, t)$ is vertical and $(t, \\frac{t - 1}{t})$ is horizontal or vice-versa. However, the denominators of the vector coordinates cannot be the same, since the first one we wrote would give us $(2, -1)$ and the other one $(1, 2),$ which wouldn't really make any sense. Since this is the only remaining case, the answer is $\\boxed{\\text{C}}$. The answer is \\text{C}",
